/* lode's app-level Textual stylesheet (lode-1i8.4).
 *
 * Before this file the repo had no TUI CSS at all -- every screen rendered
 * with Textual's bare unstyled layout. Most visibly,
 * :class:`~lode.tui.screens.capture.DiscardConfirmScreen`'s Save/Discard/
 * Cancel prompt had no sizing or centering, so it read as a blank full
 * screen with a floating line of text rather than a popup dialog.
 *
 * :class:`~textual.screen.ModalScreen`'s own ``DEFAULT_CSS`` already dims the
 * screen underneath (``background: $background 60%``) -- confirmed against
 * the installed Textual version (8.2.8) before writing this file, per the
 * ticket's design note -- so the only gap this file closes is sizing and
 * centering the dialog box itself; the editor stays visible (dimmed) behind
 * it for free.
 *
 * Loaded via ``LodeApp.CSS_PATH`` (:mod:`lode.tui.app`) as an *external*
 * stylesheet (not ``DEFAULT_CSS``) -- this file is the repo's first, and the
 * pattern every future screen's styling should extend rather than
 * duplicate. It ships with the package via
 * ``[tool.setuptools.package-data]`` in ``pyproject.toml``.
 */

DiscardConfirmScreen {
    align: center middle;
}

#capture-confirm-dialog {
    width: 50;
    height: auto;
    border: thick $primary;
    background: $panel;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* Enrichment inspector modal (lode-ay5.2) -- a larger, scrollable popup
 * (unlike #capture-confirm-dialog's small fixed box) since a note's summary/
 * tags/entities/edges can run longer than the terminal height. Height is
 * bounded (not auto) so the container itself -- not the dialog growing past
 * the screen -- is what scrolls.
 */
EnrichmentModalScreen {
    align: center middle;
}

#enrichment-inspector-dialog {
    width: 80%;
    height: 80%;
    border: thick $primary;
    background: $panel;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* Browse's delete confirm (lode-d32.1) -- same popup treatment as capture's
 * DiscardConfirmScreen above, just its own screen/id pair since the two
 * dialogs' prompts and choices differ. */
DeleteConfirmScreen {
    align: center middle;
}

#delete-confirm-dialog {
    width: 50;
    height: auto;
    border: thick $primary;
    background: $panel;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* Related-notes panel (lode-olmi.9) -- a visible border once Ctrl+F moves
 * focus onto it, so "you can now step through results" has an on-screen cue
 * rather than relying purely on the in-text "▸" selection marker. */
RelatedNotesPanel:focus {
    border: round $accent;
}

/* Related-note highlighted-context modal (lode-olmi.9) -- same larger,
 * scrollable popup treatment as #enrichment-inspector-dialog above, since a
 * note's full body can run longer than the terminal height. */
RelatedNoteModalScreen {
    align: center middle;
}

#related-note-modal-dialog {
    width: 80%;
    height: 80%;
    border: thick $primary;
    background: $panel;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* Content-viewer modal (lode-0sjj) -- same larger, scrollable popup
 * treatment as #enrichment-inspector-dialog / #related-note-modal-dialog
 * above, since a retrieved page's extracted body (or raw HTML) can run much
 * longer than the terminal height; the TextArea inside scrolls its own
 * content within this bounded box. */
SnapshotViewerScreen {
    align: center middle;
}

#snapshot-viewer-dialog {
    width: 80%;
    height: 80%;
    border: thick $primary;
    background: $panel;
    padding: 1 2;
}

/* Tags screen (lode-olmi.6) -- top panel (tag multi-select) is capped to 30%
 * of the available height so the bottom panel (the filtered notes list)
 * keeps most of the vertical space; each panel scrolls its own overflow
 * independently. This is a deliberate override of the blanket DataTable
 * rule below -- #tags-tag-list is itself a DataTable, and the ID selector's
 * higher specificity wins over the element selector regardless of rule
 * order. */
#tags-tag-list {
    height: 30%;
    border: solid $primary;
}

/* Every DataTable gets a bounded height structurally (lode-efn2), rather
 * than requiring each screen's author to remember a per-id 1fr rule -- that
 * per-id mechanism missed two tables outright (version-history-table,
 * external-picker-table, src/lode/tui/screens/browse.py) before this
 * collapse, on top of the two it *did* cover only after separate bug
 * reports (#browse-table/lode-juz8.2, #config-knobs/lode-l38d.2). Without a
 * height constraint, a DataTable auto-sizes to its full row count, and none
 * of the containing Verticals in this app are scroll containers -- so the
 * overflowing rows run past the docked Footer and become UNREACHABLE, not
 * merely scrolled-away (Screen.max_scroll_y stays 0; corrected diagnosis
 * from lode-l38d.2's review, which had first mis-described this as "the
 * screen scrolls"). Capping every table to the remaining space (1fr) makes
 * it scroll its own rows internally instead (DataTable is a ScrollView), so
 * every row stays reachable at any terminal height regardless of which
 * screen the table lives on.
 *
 * Re-verified safe against the tree as of lode-efn2: every DataTable in
 * this app (six: #tags-tag-list, #tags-notes-table, #browse-table,
 * #config-knobs, version-history-table, external-picker-table) lives in a
 * plain, non-modal Screen's top-level layout -- none of the five
 * ModalScreen dialogs in this file (DiscardConfirmScreen,
 * EnrichmentModalScreen, DeleteConfirmScreen, RelatedNoteModalScreen,
 * SnapshotViewerScreen) contain a DataTable, so this rule never reaches
 * into one of the auto/fixed-size dialog boxes above. If a future
 * DataTable is ever added inside such a box, re-check this claim rather
 * than assuming it still holds. Screens that genuinely differ (like
 * #tags-tag-list above) keep an explicit per-id override; docs/tui.md
 * records the convention for non-DataTable middle panels this rule
 * doesn't cover. */
DataTable {
    height: 1fr;
}
